# Put the convenience symlinks (bin, out, testlogs) inside the hidden .bazel/ directory instead of the checkout's root.
# LINT.IfChange(symlink_prefix)
build --symlink_prefix=.bazel/
# LINT.ThenChange(//setup_env.sh:symlink_prefix)

# --- C++ Standard & Compiler Flags ---
build --cxxopt=-std=c++20
build --host_cxxopt=-std=c++20
# The headers of external repositories (Abseil, protobuf, googletest, and the system_libs wrappers of Eigen, Pinocchio,
# ...) reach first-party code as -isystem instead of -iquote / -I: GCC then reports the warnings of
# bazel/copts.bzl in first-party code, not in thousands of third-party lines that bury it. lib/ocs2 does the same for
# its vendored headers (system_include_paths in lib/ocs2/BUILD.bazel).
# LINT.IfChange(external_include_paths)
build --features=external_include_paths
# LINT.ThenChange(//bazel/copts.bzl:first_party_copts)
# Pinocchio's Boost and urdfdom defines are not set here: the @pinocchio target carries them as `defines`
# (bazel/system_libs.bzl), so they reach exactly the targets that depend on Pinocchio.

# --- Parallelism & Resource Limits ---
# Parallelism is bounded by MEMORY, not by cores. A translation unit that includes Pinocchio, CppAD and OCS2 peaks at
# 2-3 GB, so a fixed job count that suits one machine overcommits the next: the old `--jobs=12` put twelve of them next
# to the simulator, the 3D visualizer and the tests' own CppAD code generation on a 32 GB workstation, which went into
# swap and had to be power-cycled. One action per 4 GB of RAM (HOST_RAM is in MB): 7 on 32 GB, 3 on a 16 GB CI runner.
# Tests get half of that, because a test that tapes a CppAD model runs a compiler of its own.
# LINT.IfChange(bazel_memory_bound)
build --jobs=HOST_RAM*.00025
test --local_test_jobs=HOST_RAM*.000125
# LINT.ThenChange(//tools/resource_limits/test_resource_limits.py:bazel_memory_bound, //AGENTS.md:build_memory, //tools/ci_local.sh:runner_jobs)
# A machine with more headroom can raise it in a git-ignored user.bazelrc, e.g. `build --jobs=HOST_RAM*.0004`.
try-import %workspace%/user.bazelrc

# --- Default Compilation Mode (Release) ---
# Override with: bazel build -c dbg //...
build --compilation_mode=opt

# --- Linking ---
build --linkopt=-lpthread
build --linkopt=-ldl

# --- Sandboxing ---
# The tests that tape CppAD models compile and load them under /tmp.
build --sandbox_writable_path=/tmp

# --- Environment Variables ---
# Every variable passed here is part of every action's cache key, so only what an action needs is passed:
# - PATH, for the tools the actions run (cmake and make for rules_foreign_cc's MuJoCo build, the compiler);
# - HOME, which some of those tools read their configuration from;
# - the CUDA / NVIDIA device selection of a GPU container (docker-compose.gpu.yaml).
# Deliberately not passed:
# - LD_LIBRARY_PATH, here or to the tests: every library is found through the RUNPATH Bazel links with, and a value
#   from the shell would outrank it (robotpkg's /opt/openrobots/lib, for one, holds a libblasfeo.so of another version);
# - PYTHONPATH: the hermetic interpreter must not import what a shell points at (robotpkg's Python 3.12 bindings);
# - CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH: the MuJoCo build must not find robotpkg's CMake packages.
# The tests and tools read their data files from their runfiles (robot_runtime/robot_core/include/robot_core/
# ResourcePaths.h), so no variable of the shell locates them.
build --action_env=PATH
build --action_env=HOME
build --action_env=CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES
build --action_env=NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES

# --- Bazel 9.x compatibility ---
# Auto-load removed built-in rules (cc_library, cc_binary, etc.) for external
# deps that haven't been updated yet (e.g., googletest)
build --incompatible_autoload_externally=+cc_library,+cc_binary,+cc_test,+cc_import,+cc_shared_library

# --- rules_foreign_cc ---
build --allow_unresolved_symlinks

# --- Convenience Aliases ---
# Debug build
build:dbg --compilation_mode=dbg
build:dbg --copt=-g
build:dbg --strip=never

# Release with debug info
build:relwithdebinfo --compilation_mode=opt
build:relwithdebinfo --copt=-g
build:relwithdebinfo --strip=never

# --- clang-tidy (tools/clang_tidy/README.md): a Bazel aspect, so it runs inside --jobs and the machine lock ---
# No --jobs and no --keep_going here: the Makefile's lint-tidy targets pass --keep_going, and --jobs stays the RAM bound
# above (tools/resource_limits/test_resource_limits.py). The aspect does not change the build configuration, so the
# analysis cache and the generated files are shared with plain builds. clang-tidy-fix lints with the same checks and
# also builds their fix-its.
# LINT.IfChange(clang_tidy_config)
build:clang-tidy --aspects=//tools/clang_tidy:clang_tidy.bzl%clang_tidy_aspect
build:clang-tidy --output_groups=clang_tidy
build:clang-tidy-fix --aspects=//tools/clang_tidy:clang_tidy.bzl%clang_tidy_aspect
build:clang-tidy-fix --output_groups=clang_tidy,clang_tidy_fixes
# LINT.ThenChange(//Makefile:lint_tidy, //.github/workflows/build_test.yml:lint_tidy, //tools/ci_local.sh:lint_tidy)

# --- Test Configuration ---
test --test_output=errors
test --test_verbose_timeout_warnings
test --test_env=CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES
